From: Cubie A7S bringup <local>
Subject: [PATCH] media: sunxi: bound the combo-PHY PLL lock wait in the PCIe 100M init


sunxi_cadence_phy_pcie_internal_100m_init() waits for the combo PHY PLL to
lock with an unbounded loop:

        val = readl(combo1->top_reg + 0x900);
        while (true) {
                udelay(100);
                val = readl(combo1->top_reg + 0x900);
                if (val & BIT(0))
                        break;
        }

There is no settle delay and no timeout, so if the PLL never reports lock --
cold boot with a marginal supply, wrong combo mode, or a PHY that simply does
not come up -- the kernel spins here without ever yielding.  This runs from the
PCIe host probe path, so the visible effect is a board that never finishes
booting, with no log line explaining why (radxa-build/radxa-a733 issue #10 has
the NVMe variant of that failure: "link enumerated before it was stable").

Turn it into a bounded poll: 100 us per iteration, 2000 iterations (200 ms),
which is far above the ~100 us a healthy PLL needs.  On timeout, log the
register value and return early instead of continuing to program a PHY that is
not locked.

Two neighbouring "findings" from the same issue were checked against this tree
and are deliberately NOT changed here:

  * pcie-sunxi-rc.c:sunxi_pcie_host_speed_change() does wait after requesting a
    speed change -- sunxi_pcie_host_wait_for_speed_change() polls
    PORT_LOGIC_SPEED_CHANGE with LINK_WAIT_MAX_RETRIE retries and returns
    -ETIMEDOUT.  It is bounded already; the only open question is whether it
    should additionally wait for the link to retrain at the new rate, which is
    a behaviour change nobody has reproduced as a failure.

  * sunxi_msi_set_affinity() is a stub returning -EINVAL, so MSI interrupts all
    land on CPU0.  That costs interrupt distribution under random IO, not
    correctness, and a correct implementation has to forward to the GIC parent
    domain -- not something to guess at in a bring-up patch.

Baseline: the tree Armbian actually builds, orangepi-xunlong/linux-orangepi
branch orange-pi-6.6-sun60iw2, file bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c
(md5 566c295c0885…).

Signed-off-by: Cubie A7S bringup <local>
---
diff --git a/bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c b/bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c
--- a/bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c
+++ b/bsp/drivers/phy/sunxi-cadence-combophy.c
@@ -3705,6 +3705,14 @@
 };
 
 static void sunxi_cadence_phy_pcie_internal_100m_init(struct sunxi_cadence_phy *sunxi_cphy)
+/*
+ * Upper bound for the combo PHY PLL lock wait in the 100M-refclk init.
+ * The poll loop used to be unlimited; 200 ms is far above the ~100 us a
+ * healthy PLL needs, and turns a cold-boot hang into a logged error.
+ */
+#define PCIE_PLL_LOCK_POLL_US		100
+#define PCIE_PLL_LOCK_MAX_RETRIES	2000
+
 {
 	struct sunxi_cadence_combophy *combo1 = sunxi_cphy->combo1;
 	u32 val;
@@ -3708,6 +3708,7 @@
 {
 	struct sunxi_cadence_combophy *combo1 = sunxi_cphy->combo1;
 	u32 val;
+	int retries;
 
 	writel(0x01100001, combo1->top_reg + 0x4);
 
@@ -3839,12 +3839,25 @@
 	writel(val | 0x1, combo1->top_reg + 0x100);
 
 	val = readl(combo1->top_reg + 0x900);
-	while (true) {
-		udelay(100);
-		val = readl(combo1->top_reg + 0x900);
-		if (val & BIT(0))
-			break;
-	}
+	/*
+	 * Wait for the combo PHY PLL to lock.  This was an unbounded loop:
+	 * if the PLL never locks (cold boot, marginal supply, wrong combo
+	 * mode) the kernel spun here forever inside the PCIe host probe
+	 * path and the board never finished booting.  Give it a generous
+	 * but finite budget and report the failure instead of hanging.
+	 */
+	for (retries = 0; retries < PCIE_PLL_LOCK_MAX_RETRIES; retries++) {
+		if (val & BIT(0))
+			break;
+		udelay(PCIE_PLL_LOCK_POLL_US);
+		val = readl(combo1->top_reg + 0x900);
+	}
+	if (!(val & BIT(0))) {
+		dev_err(sunxi_cphy->dev,
+			"PCIE PLL lock timeout (top+0x900 = %#x), aborting 100M init\n",
+			val);
+		return;
+	}
 
 	val = readw(combo1->phy_reg + 0xa0);
 	val |= 0x220;
